+// How far to move a rectangle primitive sideways for the eye currently open.
+//
+// This exists because a rectangle is not an orthographic draw, and the difference
+// costs more in stereo than anywhere else. RT64 gives rectangles a projection type
+// of their own — Projection::Type::Rectangle, listed beside Orthographic rather
+// than inside it — and Fast3D agrees with that in its behaviour without ever
+// naming it: GfxDrawRectangle writes clip coordinates straight into
+// loaded_vertices and calls GfxSpTri1 with is_rect set, so P_matrix is never
+// consulted and StereoProjection above never runs for a single one of them.
+//
+// Left alone, then, every rectangle is emitted at identical coordinates in both
+// eyes. Identical coordinates are zero disparity, and zero disparity is a thing
+// the eyes converge on at infinity — while the world it is drawn over sits a few
+// metres away. That is precisely the fight the orthographic HUD's shift was
+// written to avoid, and it was only ever half won: much of what a player actually
+// reads mid-race in Mario Kart 64 — lap, position, the item window — arrives as a
+// texture rectangle rather than as an orthographic quad.
+//
+// Three kinds of rectangle must not be moved:
+//
+//   - Fill-cycle rectangles. G_CYC_FILL is how this game clears; init_z_buffer
+//     and its splitscreen counterpart point the colour image at the z-buffer and
+//     fill it. A clear has no distance to be placed at, and moving one leaves a
+//     band of the previous frame's contents along the edge it moved away from.
+//   - Rectangles drawn into an off-screen framebuffer. The shift belongs to the
+//     draw that reaches the wearer's eye, and that is the later one that composites
+//     this target onto the screen. Applying it here as well would place the
+//     convergence inside a texture and then converge the texture again.
+//
+// Full-width rectangles used to be a third exclusion, on the reasoning that a
+// background belongs at infinity and identity coordinates put it there. They do
+// not. Identity maps the image across each eye's *own* NDC, and the eyes' views
+// are asymmetric mirrored frustums with a rotational cant: the same coordinates
+// mean visibly different directions in the two eyes, skewed opposite ways —
+// which a wearer reported, precisely, as the main menu's backdrop being
+// "reversed" in the right eye. The blue-sky backdrop is drawn as exactly such
+// full-width strips. Full-width rectangles are still recognised — they are
+// backgrounds by construction — but they now land on the backdrop panel, which
+// is a real object in the recentre frame, consistent between the eyes, and
+// wider than the view, so the unfilled-band worry the old exclusion answered
+// does not arise.
+bool Interpreter::StereoRectOnPanel(float ndcLeft, float ndcRight, uint32_t cycleType) const {
+    (void)ndcLeft;
+    (void)ndcRight;
+    return mStereoActive && cycleType != G_CYC_FILL && !mFbActive;
+}
